{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfn1g3z5qxXqWBXD8HUe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanganate is Mn(VII), a d⁰ tetrahedral complex. The ligand-field model can't give Dq, B or C for it, so I'm not reporting numbers.\n\n1. **Origin of the bands.** With no d electrons, there are no d–d transitions. The bands at 17 570, 31 560 and 38 320 cm⁻¹ are ligand-to-metal charge-transfer (LMCT) transitions. They are O(2p) → Mn(e, t₂) transitions: 1t₁ → 2e, 1t₁ → 7t₂, and so on.\n\n2. **Intensity.** Their ε values are about 1 500–3 000 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. That is typical of dipole-allowed charge transfer. Spin- and Laporte-forbidden d–d bands in a tetrahedral complex would be much weaker.\n\n3. **Why the model fails.** Dq and B are defined by fitting d^n term energies, using Tanabe–Sugano matrices. A d⁰ ion has only one term, ¹A₁, so there are no d–d excited states to fit. The charge-transfer energies depend on orbital energies and covalency. They are not set by Dq, B or C of the metal ion.\n\n4. **Possible misuse.** Treating the three bands as d–d transitions would give numbers with no physical meaning. A fit would also be badly underdetermined, because it would use three bands to fix parameters that don't govern them. The only value that could be read from the spectrum is the 1t₁ → 2e energy of about 17 570 cm⁻¹. That is an LMCT energy, not a ligand-field splitting. Some authors do quote the 2e–7t₂ separation, roughly 31 560 − 17 570 ≈ 14 000 cm⁻¹, as an effective \"10Dq\". That figure is an empirical orbital-energy difference. It isn't a ligand-field parameter, and it can't be paired with a meaningful B.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"MnO4- is d0 and its bands are LMCT, not d-d, so Dq and B cannot be determined from a ligand-field fit.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creat